+A lifetime later the French scientist Louis Pasteur made new treatments that trained the  body to fight other diseases. In honor of Jenner's cowpox he proposed calling them all by the same  name, vaccines. So every vaccine for every disease still carries the name of a cow.  And Jenner's idea went further than he could have dreamed. In the 20th century health workers  traveled to every country where smallpox still lived, vaccinating everyone around each new case  until there was nowhere left for it to go. On the 8th of May 1980 the world's health leaders declared  that smallpox was gone. A disease that had killed hundreds of millions of people over the centuries  had been wiped out, the first ever ended by humans. Why was this so groundbreaking? Because  it showed that the body can learn to fight a disease before it ever meets it. A mild,  safe cousin of an illness can teach your body to recognize the real thing.  That one idea is behind every vaccine you have ever had. And because no one alive today needs  a vaccine against smallpox most of us have never seen it at all. The speckled monster is something  you will only ever meet in a history book, a milkmaid's hand, a country doctor and a boy who  stayed well. Together they turned a farmyard rumor into proof and gave a cow's name to medicine.  Long before Jenner another mystery frightened people every summer.  To solve it a printer in Philadelphia flew a kite into a thunderstorm.
